"# Lead-flow verification for web agencies

_Stage: new · Model: claude-x · Prompt: case-write.v1_

## Summary

**One-liner:** Daily synthetic test leads through every client site's quote form, confirmed all the way to the inbox or CRM, sold to the agency that maintains the sites.

**Buyer:** Small web and marketing agencies that maintain 20-100 small-business client sites on retainer. They, not the plumber, see the same broken-form failure across many sites, take the blame when leads stop, and need proof that their maintenance retainer is worth paying for.

**Problem:** A contact or quote form shows its success message, but a plugin update, SMTP change or CRM integration failure means the lead never arrives. Uptime monitors and analytics still report healthy, so the failure is silent until the client notices a dry pipeline weeks later.

**Solution:** One engine submits a tagged test lead through each client site's real form on a schedule and checks that it lands at the destination (inbox, CRM or webhook). When it does not arrive, the agency gets an alert naming the site and the broken step, and every month gets a per-client report proving the lead path worked.

**Price:** $8 per site per month. Priced per monitored site so it scales with the agency's book and can be passed through on the client's maintenance invoice; well under the value of a single recovered lead.

**Why now:** WordPress plugin churn and the move to authenticated email sending mean form delivery breaks more often, while agencies are under pressure to justify retainers with visible proof of work.

**Why it wins:** Uptime and browser-automation tools stop at 'the page loaded' or 'the form submitted'. This is the only level that confirms the lead reached the business, and it is sold to the aggregator who feels the pain on every client site.

**Next action:** Interview five agency owners who maintain 30+ client sites, then run a free 30-day pilot on one agency's sites and show them the first silent failure it catches.

## For you to check

- **Competitor:** Generic uptime monitor ([uptime-tool.example](https://uptime-tool.example/pricing)): the checker could not find this on the linked page — check it yourself
- **Competitor pricing:** Generic uptime monitor pricing: $15/month for 50 monitors ([uptime-tool.example](https://uptime-tool.example/pricing)): the checker could not find this on the linked page — check it yourself

## Evidence

### Signals

- "Our forms die after plugin updates" (Hacker News, [news.example](https://news.example/item/1)) ✅

### Claims

- ✅ verified: price.unitsPerBuyer: 40
  - Quote: "quote 50"
  - Source: [agency-benchmarks.example](https://agency-benchmarks.example/2026-small-agency-report)
- ✅ verified: problemCost\\[0\\].unitCost (lost leads from broken forms): 400
  - Quote: "quote 51"
  - Source: [trades-marketing.example](https://trades-marketing.example/average-lead-value-home-services)
- ✅ verified: reachableBuyers: 3000
  - Quote: "quote 52"
  - Source: [agency-directory.example](https://agency-directory.example/web-design-agencies-canada-us)

## Numbers

### Key results

- Annual price per buyer: $3,840
- Annual problem cost per buyer: $96,000
- Annual savings per buyer: $48,000
- ROI multiple: 12.5x
- Payback: 0.96 months
- Annual revenue potential: $11,520,000
- Year 1 revenue: $115,200
- Gross margin: 87.5%
- Breakeven buyers: 1

### Formulas

- Annual price = $8 × 40 × 12 = $3,840
- Annual cost of lost leads from broken forms = 6 × $400 × 40 = $96,000
- Annual problem cost = $96,000
- Annual savings = $96,000 × 0.5 = $48,000
- ROI multiple = annual savings $48,000 ÷ annual price $3,840 = 12.5×
- Payback = annual price $3,840 ÷ (annual savings $48,000 ÷ 12) = 0.96 months
- Monthly price per buyer = $3,840 / 12 = $320
- Monthly delivery cost per buyer = $40
- Gross margin = (monthly price $320 − delivery $40) ÷ $320 = 87.5%
- Annual revenue potential = 3000 × $3,840 = $11,520,000
- Year 1 revenue = $11,520,000 × 0.01 = $115,200
- Breakeven buyers = ceil($30 / ($320 - $40)) = 1

### Inputs

| Input | Value | Source |
| --- | --- | --- |
| price.amount | 8 | assumption: Our proposed price per monitored site per month, set well below the value of one lead. |
| price.unitsPerBuyer | 40 | [agency-benchmarks.example](https://agency-benchmarks.example/2026-small-agency-report): "The median agency in our survey maintains 40 client websites on a monthly care plan." ✅ |
| problemCost\\[0\\].quantity (lost leads from broken forms) | 6 | assumption: Leads lost per silent form failure before anyone notices (roughly three weeks of enquiries for a small trades business). |
| problemCost\\[0\\].unitCost (lost leads from broken forms) | 400 | [trades-marketing.example](https://trades-marketing.example/average-lead-value-home-services): "For home-service businesses, the average value of a qualified inbound lead is about $400." ✅ |
| problemCost\\[0\\].perYear (lost leads from broken forms) | 40 | assumption: One silent failure per client site per year across the agency's 40 sites. |
| expectedReduction | 0.5 | assumption: Daily checks catch failures within a day, recovering about half of the leads that would otherwise be lost. |
| reachableBuyers | 3000 | [agency-directory.example](https://agency-directory.example/web-design-agencies-canada-us): "Our directory lists 3,000 web design agencies in Canada and the US that advertise website maintenance plans." ✅ |
| captureRate | 0.01 | assumption: 1% of reachable agencies buy in year one from cold outreach. |
| deliveryCostPerBuyerMonthly\\[0\\] (infra+LLM) | 40 | assumption: Headless browser runs, test inboxes and LLM triage for 40 sites per month. |
| fixedCostsMonthly\\[0\\] (hosting) | 30 | assumption: One small VPS plus database for the scheduler. |

### Scenarios

| Buyers | MRR | ARR | Monthly gross profit | Monthly net |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 10 | $3,200 | $38,400 | $2,800 | $2,770 |
| 50 | $16,000 | $192,000 | $14,000 | $13,970 |
| 200 | $64,000 | $768,000 | $56,000 | $55,970 |

## Competitors

| Name | Link | Pricing | Gap | Product | Pricing check |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Generic uptime monitor | [uptime-tool.example](https://uptime-tool.example/pricing) | $15/month for 50 monitors | Checks the page responds; never submits the form or confirms delivery. | 🔍 needs review | 🔍 needs review |
| Synthetic browser testing platform | [synthetic-tests.example](https://synthetic-tests.example/pricing) | From $99/month | Can fill and submit a form, but stops at the success message and is priced and built for engineering teams, not agencies. | ✅ verified | ✅ verified |

### Levels

| Level | What it checks | Limitation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Uptime | Website responds | Doesn't detect broken customer journeys |
| Browser automation | Form can be filled and submitted | May not confirm the lead was delivered |
| Lead-flow verification | Customer action produces the expected downstream result | Requires some knowledge of the destination |

## Pitch

### Elevator pitch

We send a test lead through every one of your clients' quote forms each day and confirm it actually arrives. When one silently breaks, you know before your client does.

### Emails

#### Email 1: Are all 40 of your clients' forms actually delivering?

Hi \\[First name\\],

When a plugin update breaks a client's contact form, the thank-you message still shows and uptime stays green, but the leads stop. Usually the client notices first.

I run a service that sends a tagged test lead through each client site's form every day and confirms it reaches the inbox or CRM. It costs $8 per site per month.

Would a free 30-day check on your sites be useful?

\\[Your name\\]  
\\[Mailing address\\]  
\\[Phone or website\\]  
\\[Unsubscribe line\\]

#### Email 2: Proof your maintenance retainer works

Hi \\[First name\\],

Your care plans promise the site keeps working. A monthly report showing every client's lead path was tested and delivered is hard proof of that.

We test each form daily and send you the report. Happy to set it up free on a few sites.

\\[Your name\\]  
\\[Mailing address\\]  
\\[Phone or website\\]  
\\[Unsubscribe line\\]

#### Email 3: The failure your uptime monitor can't see

Hi \\[First name\\],

Uptime monitors check that a page loads. They don't check that a submitted lead reaches your client. That gap is where leads silently disappear after SMTP or CRM changes.

We close it with a daily end-to-end test per site. Worth a 15-minute call?

\\[Your name\\]  
\\[Mailing address\\]  
\\[Phone or website\\]  
\\[Unsubscribe line\\]

### Follow-ups

#### Follow-up 1: Re: client form checks

Hi \\[First name\\], just following up. If it helps, I can run one free check on your three busiest client sites and send you the result.

\\[Your name\\]  
\\[Mailing address\\]  
\\[Phone or website\\]  
\\[Unsubscribe line\\]

#### Follow-up 2: Closing the loop

Hi \\[First name\\], I won't keep emailing. If silent form failures ever become a headache, reply and I'll set up a free pilot.

\\[Your name\\]  
\\[Mailing address\\]  
\\[Phone or website\\]  
\\[Unsubscribe line\\]

### Call script

Open: ask how many client sites they maintain and how they find out when a form stops delivering. Probe: last time a client complained about missing enquiries, how long it took to notice. Offer: free 30-day pilot on their sites with a daily delivery test and an end-of-month report. Close: book the pilot setup call.

### Objections

- **We already have uptime monitoring.**: Uptime confirms the page loads; this confirms the lead arrives. They catch different failures.
- **Our forms rarely break.**: The pilot is free, so you lose nothing if it finds nothing, and you get a report to show clients either way.

### Pricing copy

$8 per monitored site per month. Daily end-to-end lead tests, instant alerts when a form stops delivering, and a monthly per-client proof report. No setup fee, cancel anytime.

